{
  "notes": [
    {
      "text": "In a 12-month randomized trial comparing alternate-day fasting (ADF) with daily calorie restriction (CR), weight loss was 6.0% with ADF versus 5.3% with CR; the difference was not significant. The trial concluded ADF did not produce superior adherence, weight loss, weight maintenance, or cardiovascular-risk improvements. Dropout was 38% with ADF versus 29% with CR.",
      "sourceIds": ["s15", "s16"]
    },
    {
      "text": "A separate zero-calorie ADF pilot reported no between-group difference in weight regain over weeks 8–32; reported body-composition changes during regain were CR: +1.2 ± 0.8 kg fat mass (FM) and +1.1 ± 0.5 kg lean mass (LM), versus ADF: −0.4 ± 0.8 kg FM (p=0.173 vs CR) and +2.0 ± 0.5 kg LM (p=0.197 vs CR). The snippet does not establish that this pilot had at least 12 months’ follow-up.",
      "sourceIds": ["s17"]
    },
    {
      "text": "In the 12-month randomized trial by Liu et al., 139 adults with obesity were assigned to time-restricted eating (TRE) plus calorie restriction (n=69; eating 8:00 a.m.–4:00 p.m.) or daily CR alone (n=70). Both groups were instructed to consume 1,500–1,800 kcal/day for men and 1,200–1,500 kcal/day for women.",
      "sourceIds": ["s14", "s22"]
    },
    {
      "text": "At 12 months in the Liu trial, mean weight change was −8.0 kg (95% CI −9.6 to −6.4) with TRE plus CR and −6.3 kg (95% CI −7.8 to −4.7) with daily CR; the net difference was −1.8 kg (95% CI −4.0 to 0.4; P=0.11), not statistically significant.",
      "sourceIds": ["s14", "s20", "s22"]
    },
    {
      "text": "In the Liu trial, 135/139 participants (97.1%) completed the 6-month intervention and 118/139 (84.9%) completed the 12-month follow-up. Adherence was defined as the mean percentage of days participants adhered to both prescribed calories and the eating period, but the available excerpt gives no adherence values.",
      "sourceIds": ["s14", "s20"]
    },
    {
      "text": "A 12-month randomized comparison of intermittent (5:2) versus continuous energy restriction in healthy overweight or obese adults is cited as Headland et al., International Journal of Obesity (2019), 43:2028–2036, DOI 10.1038/s41366-018-0247-2. The available excerpts do not provide its numerical weight outcomes, adherence, dropout, regain, or achieved-intake comparison.",
      "sourceIds": ["s12", "s13"]
    },
    {
      "text": "A 2022 meta-analysis excerpt reports that across intermittent-energy-restriction comparisons, study energy intakes were comparable between groups in 17 trials, lower in the intermittent-restriction group in 5, and unreported in 6; its included trials ranged from 2–52 weeks, so this is not itself evidence for a ≥12-month head-to-head trial.",
      "sourceIds": ["s8"]
    },
    {
      "text": "Thomas et al. (2022) compared early TRE plus CR with daily CR for 39 weeks (12-week intervention plus 27-week follow-up), shorter than the requested 12-month threshold; reported weight loss at 39 weeks was 5.2% versus 4.7%, respectively.",
      "sourceIds": ["s11"]
    }
  ],
  "openThreads": [
    "Find primary-source details for the 2017 ADF trial, including prescribed and achieved calorie intake, adherence measures, weight maintenance/regain results, and other dropout details.",
    "Retrieve the full Headland et al. 2019 trial results to extract between-group weight outcomes, regain, adherence, dropout, and whether prescribed or achieved energy intake was comparable.",
    "
